<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">French conquest of Algeria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1830">1830</a>–<a href="/years/1903">1903</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The French conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> began with the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> from its Ottoman-era regency in 1830 and continued for decades. Constantine fell in 1837, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir-abdelkader">Emir Abdelkader</a> led resistance in the west until his surrender in 1847, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabylia">Kabylia</a> was subdued in 1857, with later revolts such as that of 1871 put down. Algeria was then ruled as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and settled by Europeans until its independence in 1962.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/french-conquest-of-algeria.jpg" alt="French conquest of Algeria" width="320" height="319" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Horace Vernet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_prise_de_Constantine_1837_par_Horace_Vernet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
